Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:05.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}CAESAR DIDN'T CROSS THE RUBICON TO SAVE ROME. HE DID IT TO STAY OUT OF PRISON.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.00,0:00:12.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}IN 50 BC, THE SENATE CORNERED HIM. IF HE RETURNED WITHOUT HIS ARMY, HE FACED IMMEDIATE PROSECUTION AND TOTAL RUIN.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.00,0:00:19.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}THEY DEMANDED HE SURRENDER HIS COMMAND. BUT CAESAR VALUED HIS DIGNITAS—HIS HONOR—ABOVE THE LAW ITSELF.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.00,0:00:27.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}WITH ONLY THE 13TH LEGION, HE REACHED THE FORBIDDEN BOUNDARY. ONE STEP MEANT HIGH TREASON; STAYING MEANT POLITICAL DEATH.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.00,0:00:36.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}HE FAMOUSLY SAID 'THE DIE IS CAST,' BUT THE REALITY IS SIMPLER: CAESAR DIDN'T CHOOSE A COUP, HE CHOSE SURVIVAL.